WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.439
Hypothesis-driven hunting turns a broad concern into something an analyst can test.

00:00:04.439 --> 00:00:08.779
A vague suspicion sounds like, something may be wrong with privileged accounts.

00:00:08.779 --> 00:00:10.948
A testable hypothesis is more specific:

00:00:10.948 --> 00:00:13.841
if an adversary is using compromised privileged credentials,

00:00:13.841 --> 00:00:17.457
then authentication logs may show privileged accounts accessing unusual systems,

00:00:17.457 --> 00:00:20.350
from unusual source locations, outside normal administrative windows,

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.158
during the last fourteen days.

00:00:22.158 --> 00:00:25.051
That statement can guide data collection and analysis.

00:00:25.051 --> 00:00:27.944
A practical hunt hypothesis usually includes five parts.

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:30.837
First, suspected behavior: what activity might be happening?

00:00:30.837 --> 00:00:34.814
Second, affected assets or identities: which systems, users, groups, cloud accounts,

00:00:34.814 --> 00:00:36.984
or network segments are in scope?

00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:42.046
Third, required telemetry: which logs or sensors should show the behavior if it occurred?

00:00:42.046 --> 00:00:45.301
Fourth, expected observations: what patterns would support the hypothesis?

00:00:45.301 --> 00:00:49.279
Fifth, decision criteria: what evidence would make the analyst escalate, reject,

00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:50.725
or refine the hunt?

00:00:50.725 --> 00:00:53.618
Decision criteria are important because hunting can drift.

00:00:53.618 --> 00:00:54.341
Without criteria,

00:00:54.341 --> 00:00:59.042
an analyst may keep pivoting forever or treat any unusual event as meaningful.

00:00:59.042 --> 00:01:03.381
Criteria do not have to be rigid, but they should be explicit.

00:01:03.381 --> 00:01:04.104
For example,

00:01:04.104 --> 00:01:08.444
the hunt may escalate if a privileged account authenticates to a sensitive

00:01:08.444 --> 00:01:12.783
server from a source IP not used by that account in the

00:01:12.783 --> 00:01:16.761
prior thirty days and the activity has no approved change ticket.

00:01:16.761 --> 00:01:21.100
The hunt may reject the hypothesis if all unusual logons map to

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.270
known maintenance windows and service accounts.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.801
Hypotheses can be confirmed, rejected, or refined.

00:01:25.801 --> 00:01:28.332
Confirmed does not always mean confirmed malicious.

00:01:28.332 --> 00:01:32.310
It may mean the expected behavior was observed and needs validation.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:35.564
Rejected does not mean the environment is safe forever.

00:01:35.564 --> 00:01:41.350
It means the available evidence did not support that hypothesis in that scope and time window.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.966
Refined means the initial idea was too broad, too narrow,

00:01:44.966 --> 00:01:47.136
or pointed at the wrong telemetry,

00:01:47.136 --> 00:01:50.752
and the analyst learned enough to ask a better question.

00:01:50.752 --> 00:01:53.283
Good hypotheses are also honest about telemetry.

00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:56.899
If endpoint process data is missing from half the fleet,

00:01:56.899 --> 00:02:01.238
a hypothesis about process execution cannot be fully tested across all endpoints.

00:02:01.238 --> 00:02:03.770
If DNS logs only retain seven days,

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.747
a thirty-day hunt needs a different source or a narrower conclusion.

00:02:07.747 --> 00:02:10.640
These limitations should appear in the hunt notes.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.895
For beginners, the value of a hypothesis is focus.

00:02:13.895 --> 00:02:18.957
It tells you which data to query, which pivots are reasonable, which assumptions matter,

00:02:18.957 --> 00:02:21.488
and what outcome would change your confidence.

00:02:21.488 --> 00:02:24.743
It also makes the hunt reviewable by another analyst.

00:02:24.743 --> 00:02:29.444
A documented hypothesis turns hunting from personal intuition into a repeatable defensive workflow.

00:02:29.444 --> 00:02:33.421
Hypotheses should be small enough to test with the available data.

00:02:33.421 --> 00:02:36.676
If the suspected behavior covers every platform, every identity,

00:02:36.676 --> 00:02:39.569
and every possible adversary objective, break it down.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:43.908
A focused hypothesis about one behavior in one environment is usually more

00:02:43.908 --> 00:02:47.162
productive than a sweeping statement that cannot be validated.

00:02:47.162 --> 00:02:51.140
Scope gives the analyst a way to finish the work responsibly.
